The Three Settings That Guarantee Under 2 Minutes

These are the non-negotiables built into the directive that keep you safely inside the time budget:

1. Depth Model: ViT-S (not ViT-B)
The ViT-S model is 25MB and runs in ~4–8 seconds on M5. The ViT-B is 97MB and takes 2–3x longer. The directive already defaults to ViT-S with ViT-B as fallback only if quality is insufficient.

2. Max Resolution: 1024px
The image_ingest.py script in the directive auto-resizes any image to 1024px on its longest side before processing. This is the single biggest time saver — a 4K input without resizing would add 3–4x processing time.

3. Frame Count: 48 frames (2 seconds @ 24fps)
This is the default in the directive. Each additional second of video adds roughly 10–15 seconds of render time. If you want longer output (5–10 second loops), expect 90–150 seconds and you'd still be at or under 2.5 minutes.

One Important Caveat: Background Inpainting

The most unpredictable timing element is the background inpainting step — filling in the pixels exposed when the foreground layer shifts. If your image has a complex, textured background and a large foreground subject, the inpaint area is bigger and slower. For simple landscapes, skies, or images with small subjects, this step is fast. If inpainting is blowing your time budget, the directive's error handling kicks in and falls back to a simple blur-fill instead, saving 10–15 seconds.

